\[\mathbf{u}_{0\mu}=\mathbf{curl}(\theta_{\mu}\psi_{0})\]

where \(\theta_{\mu}\) is defined as in Lemma 2.4. Clearly \(\mathbf{u}_{0\mu}\!\in\!H^{1}(\Omega)^{3}\) and

\[\operatorname{div}\mathbf{u}_{0\mu}=0,\quad\mathbf{u}_{0\mu}\!\mid_{\Gamma}= \mathbf{g}.\]

Now, using Lemma 2.4, we have if \(d(x;\Gamma)\leqslant 2\delta(\mu)\)

\[\left|\frac{\partial(\theta_{\mu}\psi_{0i})}{\partial x_{j}}(x)\right| \leqslant\frac{\mu}{d(x;\Gamma)}|\psi_{0i}(x)|+\left|\frac{\partial\psi_{0i}}{ \partial x_{j}}(x)\right|\]

so that

\[\|\mathbf{u}_{0\mu}(x)\|\leqslant C_{1}[(\mu/d(x;\Gamma))\,\|\psi_{0}(x)\|\,+\, \|D\psi_{0}(x)\|\,]\]

where \(\|\,.\,\|\) denotes as usual the Euclidean norm of \(\mathbb{R}^{N}\) and

\[\|D\psi_{0}(x)\|=\left(\sum_{i,\,j=1}^{N}|\partial\psi_{0i}(x)/\partial x_{j} |^{2}\right)^{1/2}.\]

Let \(\mathbf{v}\) belong to \(V\). Since \(H^{2}(\Omega)\subset\mathcal{C}^{0}(\bar{\Omega})\), we obtain

\[\|v_{i}u_{0\mu j}\|_{0\,,\,\Omega}\leqslant C_{2}\left\{\mu\,\|v_{i}/d(\,.; \,\Gamma)\|_{0\,,\,\Omega}+\left(\int_{d(x;\,\Gamma)\leqslant 2\delta(\mu)}[v_{i} \|D\psi_{0}(x)\|]^{2}\,dx\right)^{1/2}\right\}.\]

Applying Lemma 2.5 gives

\[\|v_{i}/d(\,.;\,\Gamma)\|_{0\,,\,\Omega}\leqslant C_{3}|v_{i}|_{1\,,\,\Omega}.\]

Moreover, since \(H^{1}(\Omega)\subset L^{6}(\Omega)\), we have by Holder's inequality

\[\left(\int_{d(x;\,\Gamma)\leqslant 2\delta(\mu)}[v_{i}\|D\psi_{0}\|]^{2}\,dx \right)^{1/2}\leqslant C_{4}|v_{i}|_{1\,,\,\Omega}\times\left(\int_{d(x;\, \Gamma)\leqslant 2\delta(\mu)}\|D\psi_{0}(x)\|\,^{3}\,dx\right)^{1/3}.\]

Setting

\[\phi(\mu)=\left(\int_{d(x;\,\Gamma)\leqslant 2\delta(\mu)}\|D\psi_{0}(x)\| ^{3}\,dx\right)^{1/3},\]

we get

\[\|v_{i}u_{0\mu j}\|_{0\,,\,\Omega}\leqslant C_{5}(\mu\,+\,\phi(\mu))|v_{i}|_{ 1\,,\,\Omega}.\]

Therefore, using Lemma 2.2, we have

\[|a_{1}(\mathbf{v};\mathbf{u}_{0\mu},\mathbf{v})|=|a_{1}(\mathbf{v };\mathbf{v},\mathbf{u}_{0\mu})| =\left|\sum_{i,\,j=1}^{N}\int_{\Omega}v_{i}u_{0\mu j}\partial v_{j }/\partial x_{i}\,dx\right|\] \[\leqslant C_{6}(\mu\,+\,\phi(\mu))|\mathbf{v}|_{1\,,\,\Omega}^{2}.\]

Thus, given \(\varepsilon>0\) and since \(\lim_{\mu\,\,.0}\phi(\mu)=0\), we may choose \(\mu=\mu(\varepsilon)\) small enough so that

\[C_{6}(\mu\,+\,\phi(\mu))\leqslant\varepsilon.\]
